{"review":"approved","flags":[{"page":1,"note":"Scanned deck; content read from page images and OCR. All 47 pages reviewed."},{"page":32,"note":"The algorithm writes ŷ = sign(Σwᵢxᵢ + b) while the worked examples use a 0/1 step; the lesson states both conventions (p.24 says they are equivalent)."}],"recall":{"defs":[["Artificial neuron","Weighted sum plus bias passed through an activation."],["Perceptron","Single neuron with a threshold activation."],["Learning rate","Step size of weight updates."],["Linearly separable","Classes separable by a hyperplane."],["Connectionism","Intelligence from interconnected simple units."],["Epoch","One pass over the training examples."]],"formulas":["z=\\sum_{i=1}^nw_ix_i+b","\\hat y=\\begin{cases}1&z\\ge0\\\\0&z<0\\end{cases}","w_i\\leftarrow w_i+\\eta(t-\\hat y)x_i","h=\\mathbf w^T\\tilde{\\mathbf x}"],"takeaways":["Neuron: z = Σwᵢxᵢ + b, ŷ = f(z).","Perceptron = linear threshold classifier.","AND, OR, NOT via one perceptron each.","Learn by updating on mistakes; converges if separable.","XOR needs hidden layers."],"confused":[["{0,1} vs {−1,+1}","Equivalent conventions."],["Weights vs bias","Feature importance vs threshold shift."],["Perceptron vs logistic regression","Step vs sigmoid; mistakes-only vs every-example updates."],["Linearly separable vs learnable","Perceptron can learn only the former."]],"minute":"An artificial neuron, inspired by biological neurons with dendrites, soma, axon and synapses, computes a weighted sum of its inputs plus a bias and applies an activation function; its knowledge lives in the weights. Networks of such neurons in input, hidden and output layers learn by tuning weights. The perceptron (Rosenblatt, 1958) outputs 1 when the weighted sum is at least zero: a linear classifier. It can implement AND (−1, 0.75, 0.75), OR (−1, 2, 2) and NOT, learning weights by wᵢ ← wᵢ + η(t − ŷ)xᵢ on mistakes, and converges whenever the data are linearly separable. XOR is not, so it requires a multi-layer network."}